# The content repo, if it is ever checked out or copied in here — a deploy that
# clones it alongside the source is the likely way that happens. This repo is
# public and the prose is the product, so the guard is cheap and the mistake is
# not undoable. `fixture/` is the fake content, and stays tracked.
#
# Anchored to the root, and that leading slash is load-bearing. Unanchored,
# `ohara/` matches a directory of that name at any depth — including
# `crates/api/src/ohara/`, the module that reads the content repo, whose files
# then cannot be staged without `git add -f`. A guard that has to be overridden
# during ordinary work is a guard that gets overridden the once it mattered.
# A stray clone lands at the root, which is where this still catches it.
/ohara/
/content/
billing.yaml
pricing.yaml
